# Documentation Guidelines

## Documentation Structure

This project uses Docusaurus with Markdown and MDX (Markdown + React).

### Documentation Locations
- **Learning Materials**: `docs/learning/`
- **Course Materials**: `docs/courses/`
- **Brand Guidelines**: `docs/brand.md`
- **Product Requirements**: `docs/PRD.md`
- **Project README**: `README.md`
- **Changelog**: `CHANGELOG.md`

## Markdown Frontmatter

**ALWAYS** include frontmatter at the top of Markdown files:

```yaml
---
title: Page Title
description: Brief description of the page
exclude_cs: false  # Optional: true to exclude from Czech build
exclude_en: false  # Optional: true to exclude from English build
---
```

### Frontmatter Fields
- **title** (required): Page title shown in browser tab and page header
- **description** (optional): Page description for SEO and search
- **exclude_cs** (optional): Set to `true` to exclude page from Czech build
- **exclude_en** (optional): Set to `true` to exclude page from English build

## MDX - React in Markdown

### Importing Components

```tsx
---
title: Example Page
---

import APHCanvas from '@site/src/APHCanvas.tsx'

## Interactive Example

<APHCanvas name="ExampleName" width={800} height={600} />
```

### Component Usage Rules
1. Import at the top of the file (after frontmatter)
2. Use JSX syntax for components
3. Props must use JSX syntax (camelCase, curly braces for values)
4. Self-closing tags for components without children

## Interactive Examples Documentation

### Creating Example Pages

**Location**: `docs/learning/examples/{category}/{example-name}.md`

**Template**:
```md
---
title: Example Name
description: Brief description
---

import APHCanvas from '@site/src/APHCanvas.tsx'

Brief introduction to the example.

<APHCanvas 
	name="ExampleClassName"
	width={800}
	height={600}
/>

## Explanation

Detailed explanation of the example...

## Key Concepts

- Concept 1
- Concept 2

## Code Structure

Explanation of how the example is implemented...
```

### Example Categories
Organize examples by category:
- `01-pixi-intro/`: Basic Pixi.js examples
- `02-three-intro/`: Three.js examples
- `03-components/`: Component system examples
- `04-space/`: Spatial algorithms (pathfinding, quadtree, etc.)
- `05-dynamics/`: Movement and steering behaviors
- `06-physics/`: Physics engine examples (Matter.js)
- `07-graphics/`: Graphics programming (shaders, lighting, etc.)
- `08-ai/`: AI and game logic
- `09-multiplayer/`: Multiplayer and networking
- `10-other/`: Miscellaneous examples
- `11-games/`: Complete game examples

## Tutorial Documentation

### Tutorial Structure

**Location**: `docs/learning/tutorials/{tutorial-name}.md`

**Template**:
```md
---
title: Tutorial Title
description: What you'll learn
---

# Tutorial Title

## Prerequisites

What the reader should know before starting.

## Introduction

Brief overview of what the tutorial covers.

## Step 1: First Step

Explanation and code examples...

```js
// Code example
const example = 'code';
```

## Step 2: Next Step

Continue with next steps...

## Summary

What was covered and next steps.

## Further Reading

Links to related resources.
```

## Code Blocks

### Syntax Highlighting

Use language identifiers for syntax highlighting:

````md
```javascript
const example = 'JavaScript code';
```

```typescript
const example: string = 'TypeScript code';
```

```jsx
const Component = () => <div>JSX</div>;
```

```tsx
const Component: React.FC = () => <div>TSX</div>;
```

```bash
npm install package-name
```

```css
.class {
	property: value;
}
```
````

### Supported Languages
JavaScript, TypeScript, JSX, TSX, HTML, CSS, SCSS, Bash, JSON, Markdown, and more.

## Links

### Internal Links
```md
[Link text](/docs/learning/intro)
[Relative link](./other-page)
```

### External Links
```md
[External link](https://example.com)
```

### Reference Style Links
```md
[Link text][reference]

[reference]: https://example.com
```

## Images

### Image Locations
- **Documentation images**: `static/img/docs/{category}/`
- **Page images**: `static/img/pages/`
- **Global images**: `static/img/global/`

### Image Syntax
```md
![Alt text](/img/docs/category/image.png)
```

### Best Practices
- Use descriptive alt text
- Optimize images before adding
- Use appropriate formats (PNG for screenshots, SVG for diagrams)
- Keep images in appropriate subdirectories

## Tables

```md
| Column 1 | Column 2 | Column 3 |
|----------|----------|----------|
| Value 1  | Value 2  | Value 3  |
| Value 4  | Value 5  | Value 6  |
```

## Admonitions (Callouts)

Docusaurus supports special callout boxes:

```md
:::note
This is a note
:::

:::tip
This is a tip
:::

:::info
This is info
:::

:::caution
This is a caution
:::

:::danger
This is a danger warning
:::
```

## Sidebar Organization

### Auto-Generated Sidebar
The sidebar is automatically generated from the file structure in `docs/`.

### Sidebar Configuration
Defined in `sidebars.js` but uses auto-generation with locale filtering.

### File Organization Best Practices
1. Group related files in subdirectories
2. Use numbered prefixes for ordering (e.g., `01-intro.md`, `02-resources.md`)
3. Use descriptive kebab-case names
4. Create index files for categories if needed

## Slides Documentation

### Slides Listing Component

**Location**: `docs/learning/lectures.tsx`

This React component reads `static/slides/slides-info.json` and displays available slides.

**Features**:
- Automatically groups slides by category
- Filters by current locale
- Provides links to slide HTML files
- Includes PDF export and presentation mode links

### Slides Integration
Slides are external (downloaded at build time) and integrated via the `lectures.tsx` component.

**NEVER**:
- Manually edit slides in `static/slides/`
- Modify slides-info.json directly (it's downloaded)

**ALWAYS**:
- Update slides in the external repository
- Update `slides_version` in `web.config.js`
- Rebuild to pull new slides

## Resources Documentation

### Resources Listing Component

**Location**: `docs/learning/resources.tsx`

**Data Source**: `src/internals/resources-data.ts`

### Adding New Resources
1. Edit `src/internals/resources-data.ts`
2. Add resource object with:
   - Title
   - Description
   - URL
   - Category
   - Tags (optional)
3. Rebuild and test

## Localization in Documentation

### Czech vs English Content

**Separate Translations**:
Translation files in `i18n/{locale}/docusaurus-plugin-content-docs/current/`

**Example**:
- English: `docs/learning/intro.md`
- Czech: `i18n/cs/docusaurus-plugin-content-docs/current/learning/intro.md`

### Excluding Content by Locale

```yaml
---
title: English Only Page
exclude_cs: true
---
```

### Translation Best Practices
1. Maintain parallel structure between locales
2. Use appropriate technical terms for each language
3. Keep code examples consistent across locales
4. Test both locales after changes

## Changelog Maintenance

**Location**: `CHANGELOG.md`

### Format
Use Keep a Changelog format:

```md
# Changelog

## [Version] - YYYY-MM-DD

### Added
- New features

### Changed
- Changes to existing functionality

### Deprecated
- Soon-to-be removed features

### Removed
- Removed features

### Fixed
- Bug fixes

### Security
- Security updates
```

### When to Update
- Every version bump
- Significant feature additions
- Breaking changes
- Important bug fixes
- Dependency updates

## README Maintenance

**Location**: `README.md`

### What to Include
- Project overview
- Quick start instructions
- Build and development commands
- Project structure (high-level)
- Links to detailed documentation
- Deployment information
- License and contact

### What NOT to Include
- Detailed API documentation (use docs/ instead)
- Implementation details (use PRD.md)
- Extensive tutorials (use docs/learning/tutorials/)

### Update Triggers
Update README.md when:
- Setup process changes
- New major features added
- Build commands change
- Deployment URLs change
- Project structure changes significantly

## PRD Maintenance

**Location**: `docs/PRD.md`

This is the **single source of truth** for project architecture and requirements.

### What to Include
- Complete project overview
- Technical architecture
- Detailed feature documentation
- Build and deployment processes
- Code patterns and conventions
- Configuration reference
- External dependencies
- Known limitations

### Update Frequency
**CRITICAL**: Update PRD.md whenever:
- Architecture changes
- New features are added
- Dependencies are updated
- Build process changes
- Configuration changes
- Code patterns change
- External integrations change

### Update Process
1. Make code changes first
2. Identify affected PRD sections
3. Update with accurate, current information
4. Update "Last Updated" date at bottom
5. Review for consistency and completeness

## Documentation Quality Standards

### Writing Style
- **Clear and concise**: Avoid unnecessary jargon
- **Active voice**: "Click the button" not "The button should be clicked"
- **Present tense**: "The function returns" not "The function will return"
- **Second person**: "You can configure" not "One can configure"

### Code Examples
- **Working examples**: Always test code before documenting
- **Complete examples**: Include necessary imports and context
- **Comments**: Explain non-obvious code
- **Consistent style**: Follow project code conventions

### Structure
- **Logical flow**: Organize from general to specific
- **Clear headings**: Use descriptive heading text
- **Lists**: Use for multiple related items
- **Tables**: Use for structured data comparison

### Accessibility
- **Alt text**: Always provide for images
- **Descriptive links**: "Read the installation guide" not "Click here"
- **Clear language**: Avoid idioms that don't translate well

## Search Optimization

### Searchable Content
The local search plugin indexes:
- Page titles
- Headings
- Body content
- Parent categories

### Optimizing for Search
1. **Use descriptive titles**: Clear, specific page titles
2. **Meaningful headings**: Headings that describe content
3. **Keywords**: Include relevant technical terms naturally
4. **Hierarchy**: Proper heading levels (h1, h2, h3)

### Boost Factors
- Title: 5x boost
- Content: 1x boost
- Parent categories: 2x boost

## Common Documentation Tasks

### Adding a New Page
1. Create Markdown file in appropriate `docs/` subdirectory
2. Add frontmatter (title, description)
3. Write content using MDX if needed
4. Add images to `static/img/docs/`
5. Test in local development
6. Update PRD.md if significant

### Adding an Interactive Example
1. Ensure example exists in external examples repository
2. Create page in `docs/learning/examples/{category}/`
3. Import and use APHCanvas component
4. Document the example thoroughly
5. Test the example renders correctly
6. Update PRD.md if adding new category

### Updating External References
1. Update version in `web.config.js`
2. Run `npm run download_data`
3. Test locally
4. Update PRD.md with new version
5. Document in CHANGELOG.md

### Translating Content
1. Create parallel structure in `i18n/{locale}/`
2. Translate text and descriptions
3. Keep code examples consistent
4. Test both locales
5. Verify sidebar generates correctly

## Documentation Review Checklist

Before committing documentation changes:

- [ ] Frontmatter is complete and accurate
- [ ] Links work correctly (no broken links)
- [ ] Images display properly
- [ ] Code examples are tested and working
- [ ] Syntax highlighting is correct
- [ ] MDX components render correctly
- [ ] Content is clear and well-organized
- [ ] Spelling and grammar are correct
- [ ] Both locales tested (if applicable)
- [ ] PRD.md updated (if needed)
- [ ] README.md updated (if needed)
- [ ] CHANGELOG.md updated (if version change)
